fix(make): link the repository, never the tree the command was run from (#240)

Bring-up chose the directory to make signable with `git rev-parse
--show-toplevel`, which answers the tree you are STANDING IN. Run from inside
a linked worktree it therefore linked the worktree.

The damage was never local. The link is one symlink that every caller on the
machine resolves through, so signing then failed for everybody, including
from the main checkout, and surfaced later and elsewhere as a doubled path in
an unrelated caller's signing error. Hit twice in one day.

`git worktree list` reports the main worktree first from inside any linked
one, which is the rule mainWorktreeOf already uses in Go. That rule now lives
in one script, which both the Makefile and the link step read, so the two
cannot disagree about which tree is the repository.

The link step also REFUSES a worktree by name and says which repository to
link instead, because bring-up is not its only caller: anything passing DIR
by hand can still name one.

Driven by OPS-040 and OPS-041, written first and observed failing. The last
case of the selftest is what keeps the rest honest — a plain repository must
get PAST the guard, or a guard that refused every path would pass too.

